#
# The complete DT_NEEDED list of the flag-on wrapper libMIOpen.so, checked for
# exact equality by check_public_abi.py check-wrapper --needed-baseline.
#
# Two things this is here to catch, both of which a symbol-set check misses:
#
#   - libMIOpen_private.so.1 must be present. Without it the wrapper's stubs
#     have nothing to forward to, and a consumer that links only -lMIOpen gets
#     a library that loads and then fails at the first call.
#
#   - libhipdnn_backend.so must NOT appear. The wrapper links the frontend's
#     dynamic-loading variant, which dlopens the backend at first use, so a
#     machine without hipDNN installed still loads libMIOpen.so. A direct
#     DT_NEEDED on the backend would make hipDNN a hard install dependency of
#     every MIOpen consumer, which is the one outcome this whole design exists
#     to avoid.
#
# Exact equality rather than a pair of substring tests because a new runtime
# dependency appearing in the thin wrapper is itself worth a second look -- it
# has three translation units and should need almost nothing. The comgr,
# rocblas and sqlite3 entries are not used by any of them; they arrive from the
# private library's link interface.
#
# ld-linux is here because the wrapper has thread-local state. A thread_local in
# a shared library compiles to a call to __tls_get_addr, which lives in the
# loader, not in libc. Dropping the last thread_local would drop this entry.
#
# Regenerate from a flag-on build with:
#   script/check_public_abi.py dump <build>/lib/libMIOpen.so.1 /tmp/wrapper
# and copy the entries from /tmp/wrapper.needed. The list is platform-dependent
# (ROCm soversions, and whether the toolchain still emits libdl), so a
# regeneration that only shuffles those is routine; one that adds a new library
# is not.

libMIOpen_private.so.1
libamd_comgr.so.3
librocblas.so.5
libamdhip64.so.7
libsqlite3.so.0
libstdc++.so.6
libm.so.6
libgcc_s.so.1
libc.so.6
ld-linux-x86-64.so.2
